Reference output of the real who for clifr's tests (2026-11-20).

gnu-8.32.txt: GNU coreutils who 8.32 in an ubuntu:22.04 container (rockylinux:9's 8.32 and
rockylinux:8's 8.30 printed the same), from run.sh: gen.py's utmp as /var/run/utmp and
terminals /dev/tty7 (accessed 30 s before NOW, mode 620), tty8 (7500 s, 600) and ttyS9
(3 days, 620), TZ=UTC. utmp-lines.txt: the same records as clifrd's "utmp" source lines.

uutils-0.10.0.txt: Ubuntu 26.04's who (uutils 0.10.0, logind) on the desktop at NOW, TZ
Australia/Adelaide (ACDT, +10:30); its AppArmor profile denies it /etc/passwd, so uids. The
sessions, terminal and boot time are in uutils-logind.txt.

w-3.3.15.txt, w-3.3.17-rocky9.txt, w-3.3.17-ubuntu.txt: procps-ng w in rockylinux:8, rockylinux:9
and ubuntu:22.04 containers, from wrun.sh: two real pty sessions (script), a utmp written by
utmpgen.go.txt (utmpgen.go: built static, mounted at /ref/gen/utmpgen) naming them, a second user
on one, a stale pid and an X display; then each process's stat fields (pid ppid tty_nr pgrp tpgid
euid ruid starttime utime+stime state comm|cmdline), the terminals (stat: atime, mode, hex
major/minor) and the utmp bytes, TZ=UTC. The tests compare the lines after the uptime line.

w-4.0.4-ubuntu.txt: Ubuntu 26.04's w (procps-ng 4.0.4-9ubuntu1, logind) on the desktop with a
machinectl login (pts/2) and the GNOME session (tty2), then the process, terminal and logind
session state it read (w4cap: the same dump as wrun.sh plus /run/systemd/sessions as clifrd's
"logind" lines), trimmed to the sessions' processes and leaders, the user renamed testusr.

last-2.32.1.txt, last-2.37.4.txt, last-2.37.2.txt: util-linux last in rockylinux:8, rockylinux:9 and
ubuntu:22.04 containers, from lastrun.sh: a wtmp written by wtmpgen.go.txt (three boots: the first
crashed, the second shut down, the third running; logins ended by a logout, a crash, a shutdown,
a reused terminal, one alive (pid 1, user ghost whose uid is the containers' loginuid) and one
gone), last's output for 14 option sets (-t and -s at NOW-2 and NOW-3 days), then the wtmp bytes.
